  "hints": [
    "Phones can't keep asking \"any new messages?\" every second. What keeps a connection open so the server can push?",
    "Put a WebSocket Gateway in front of the Chat Service. Writes go into a durable log (Kafka) that feeds a write-optimized store (Cassandra).",
    "Size it: about 4 gateway replicas and 20 chat servers keep everything under 70%. Add a cache for presence and recent history, with 2 replicas."
  ],
  "scoring": {
    "weights": {
      "performance": 0.35,
      "reliability": 0.3,
      "cost": 0.15,
      "architecture": 0.2
    },
    "latencyTargetMs": 45,
    "costBudget": 3500
  },
  "explanation": [
    "Polling wastes most requests on \"nothing new\". A WebSocket stays open, so the server pushes each message the moment it arrives.",
    "Writing every message into a durable log (Kafka) decouples sending from storing and fan-out. If the database slows down, messages wait in the log instead of being lost.",
    "Chat history is append-heavy and keyed by conversation: a wide-column store like Cassandra partitions it naturally (partition key = conversation id, clustering key = timestamp).",
    "Real systems also track which gateway each user is connected to (often in Redis) so a message can be routed to the right connection."
  ]
}
